## INTRODUCTION
Luminescence-based methods are highly attractive for chemical sensing and offer several potential advantages such as low limit of detection, high sensitivity, and fast response. Particularly desirable, yet still challenging to achieve, are “turn-on” sensors that luminesce only in the presence of analyte and are non-emissive in the absence of analyte.1 “Turn-on” sensors, in contrast to  luminescence “turnoff” sensors2 where the presence of analyte attenuates an existing luminescence intensity, have significantly less luminescence background, and are therefore potentially more sensitive and reliable.3-4
The metal-centered photoluminescence (PL) of lanthanide $\left(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\right)$ compounds, such as metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), complexes, and simple salts, offers unique opportunities for chemical sensing in both solid state and solution.5-10 “Turn-on” sensing mechanisms,11-18 though under-tapped for Ln compounds, are especially attractive. Since the 4f-4f electron transitions associated with $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ PL are Laporte forbidden, direct excitation of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ 4f electron transitions is prohibitively inefficient. The excitation of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ PL is typically achieved via the “antenna effect”,19 a sensitization process in which chromophore molecules with large molar absorption coefficients harvest light and transfer energy to excite 4f electrons of the nearby $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ center. Leveraging the “antenna effect”, luminescence “turn-on” sensing with high specificity is possible for analytes that can either i) directly function as an antenna,11-12 or ii) effectively modulate the sensitization of the integral sensitizer,20 such that with a properly selected excitation wavelength $\mathrm{Ln}^{\mathrm{3+}}\mathrm{PL}$ is off in the absence of the analyte and switched on only in the presence of the analyte. Despite the pressing need for facile methods to detect a large variety of organic compounds, including pharmaceutical contaminants,21-22 industrial chemicals,23 polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons,24 biomarkers,25 and naturally occurring toxins,22 and the fact that many of these target analytes are aromatic chromophores that could be effective sensitizers for $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ PL, $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ -based luminescence “turn-on” sensors have seldomly been explored for sensing organic molecules, with few successful examples reported.11-12
In this work, we aimed to develop $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ -based luminescence “turnon” sensors for gossypol (Gsp) (Figure 1), a polyphenolic toxin concentrated in cottonseeds. The presence of Gsp causes great concern for the safe consumption of cottonseed products, including edible vegetable oil and animal feed materials. Gsp has been shown to have a series of detrimental effects in humans and animals, including acute poisoning, hepatoxicity, infertility, and immunotoxicity.26 Therefore, cottonseed products must be refined to remove Gsp. Facile and sensitive detection methods are necessary to ensure that Gsp concentrations are at safe levels in various cottonseed products, such as cottonseed oil, used commonly in the food industry, and animal feed materials. The Chinese Ministry of Health requires that the concentration of free Gsp in edible cottonseed oil not exceed $200\ \mathrm{ppm.}^{27}$ The maximum free Gsp concentrations permitted by the European Union for various animal feed materials and complete feedingstuffs range from $20~\mathrm{ppm}$ to ${5000}\mathrm{ppm}$ .28 Ultraviolet (UV) spectrophotometry methods29-33 have been extensively studied and broadly used for Gsp detection and quantification. These methods, however, are nonspecific, and light absorption of impurities can present interference. To improve selectivity, UV spectrophotometry is often coupled with highperformance liquid chromatography (HPLC).34-38 Recently, a variety of other methods have also been explored, including Fouriertransform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR),39 near infrared (NIR) spectroscopy40, piezoelectric imprinting,41 and luminescence “turnoff” sensing.42 Luminescence “turn-on” sensors for Gsp, to the best of our knowledge, have not been reported. Herein, we demonstrate the first luminescence “turn-on” sensors for Gsp using ${\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}$ MOFs and simple ${\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}$ salts.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
At the outset of this study, we surmised that Gsp might serve as an ideal sensitizer for ${\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}{\mathrm{PL}},$ because i) it is a yellow pigment that strongly absorbs UV and visible light,43 and ii) the multiple hydroxyl substituents and aldehyde groups of its two naphthalene cores are potential polydentate binding sites for $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ .44 We envisioned that Gsp could sensitize $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ and thus turn on $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ PL via two possible pathways. In the first, Gsp could either coordinate or be in proximity of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ to facilitate energy transfer. In the second scenario, the aromatic aldehyde groups of Gsp could react with aminefunctionalized aromatic linkers in $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ MOFs, the integral antennae, to form Schiff base products and effectively red-shift the sensitization wavelength. Both possible cases would allow for the sensitization of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ PL at longer wavelengths in the presence of Gsp.
Based on the above hypotheses, we synthesized a MOF analogue of the well-established $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ fcu MOF platform46-47 using ${3,3"}$ - diamino-1,1':4',1''-terphenyl- $4{,}4{^{\prime\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ - TPDC) and ${\mathrm{Yb}}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{\cdot}$ , denoted as $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC (Figure 2A). We contend that $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ -centered NIR emission is advantageous for molecular sensing,48 as it does not overlap with sample fluorescence in the visible spectrum; deliberate employment of $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ MOFs as luminescence “turn-on” sensors is unprecedented. The anticipated crystal structure of Yb- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC with fcu topology was confirmed by single crystal X-ray diffraction data (Figure 2A). The experimental powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of the bulk sample closely matched the simulated PXRD pattern of ${\mathrm{Yb-NH}}_{2}$ - TPDC, indicating high phase purity (Figure S1). The $[\mathrm{Yb}_{6}(\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mathrm{COO})_{12}]^{2-}$ secondary building units (SBUs) make Yb$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC a negatively charged framework. The charge-balancing cation, dimethylammonium, was observed in the proton nuclear magnetic resonance $\mathrm{^{1}H\ N M R})$ spectrum of the digested sample (Figure S2). With equilateral triangular pore apertures that measure $13.5\mathrm{\AA}$ in altitude, when accounting for Van der Waals radii (Figure 2B), $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC should allow entry of Gsp, which measures approximately $10.4\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\mathrm{x}10.7\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\mathrm{x}16.9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (Figure 2C). A coordinating oxygen atom, likely either from a water or dimethyl formamide (DMF) molecule, was observed near each $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ ion of the SBU (Figure S3), indicating a possible coordination site for Gsp molecules. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA; Figure S4) showed a steep weight loss below $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ corresponding to evaporation of solvent guests and a more gradual weight loss after $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The lack of a well-defined plateau signifies poor thermal stability above 120 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Yb- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC was successfully activated under vacuum at room temperature without losing crystallinity (Figure S5) after stepwise solvent exchange with dichloromethane and n-pentane.49 $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption analysis at 77K of activated sample yielded a type I isotherm (Figure S6) from which the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of $2370\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ was determined.
In the absence of Gsp, a solid ${\mathrm{Yb}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ -TPDC sample under acetone, when excited between $300{-}475\ \mathrm{\nm}$ , exhibited the characteristic $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ NIR emission band at $940\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~-~}~}1060\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~nm}~}$ corresponding to the $^{2}\mathrm{F}_{5/2}\rightarrow^{2}\mathrm{F}_{7/2}$ transition of $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ (Figure 3A, Figure S7, and Supporting Information, Page S12). Since $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ does not have electronic levels that can be excited with ultraviolet or visible light, the $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL of $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC must be due to the “antenna effect” of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC linker. Notably, the excitation band of $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC $(300~-~475~\mathrm{{\nm})}$ ) was red-shifted in comparison to the UV-vis absorption spectrum of $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC in solution, which showed two bands centered at $300\mathrm{nm}$ and 360 nm and no significant absorption at wavelengths above $410\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S8). We have observed similar effects in a different MOF.51 This large discrepancy may be due to confinement effects, where the
MOF architecture forces molecules to adopt different conformations than those in solution. Intriguingly, incubating Yb$\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -TPDC with Gsp led to a red-shift of the long wavelength edge of the excitation band of $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL from $475\ \mathrm{nm}$ as observed in the blank sample to $550~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 3B, Figure S7, and Supporting Information, Page S12). Compared to the blank sample, a decrease in $\mathrm{Yb^{3+}P L}$ intensity in the presence of Gsp was also noted when Yb$\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -TPDC was excited between 340 and $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ . We ascribe this to the attenuation of incident light caused by the strong light absorption of Gsp in solution in this range (Figure S8). These results indicate that under excitation between $475\mathrm{nm}$ and $550\mathrm{nm}$ , the $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL of ${\mathrm{Yb}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ -TPDC can be switched on from a complete nonemissive state in response to the presence of Gsp.
The observed extension of the excitation range of $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL to longer wavelength in the presence of Gsp is consistent with both of our hypothesized mechanisms for  “turn-on” detection: Gsp could either i) directly act as a sensitizer for the $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ ions, or ii) modify the sensitization wavelength of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC linker via formation of Schiff base product. We performed several studies to validate whether these mechanisms were feasible. Since both require Gsp adsorption into the MOF, we first experimentally confirmed that Gsp could indeed enter the MOF cavities.  A solution of Gsp was mixed with an activated sample of $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC, and the Gsp concentration was monitored using UV-vis spectroscopy (Figure S9). The Gsp concentration in solution steadily decreased over time, indicating likely association with the solid $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC sample. The BET surface area of a sample of ‘Gsp-loaded’ Yb- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ -TPDC, calculated from the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm (Figure S10), was determined to be $\sim1280~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ nearly half that of neat $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ - TPDC, which can be attributed to pore occlusion by adsorbed Gsp. Importantly, Yb-NH2-TPDC maintains its original crystallinity upon exposure to Gsp (Figure S11). We also conducted computational studies to support the notion that Gsp can enter Yb$\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -TPDC cavities. Pore window dimensions (videsupra) are sufficiently large to allow diffusion of Gsp into the cavities. Configurational-bias Monte Carlo (CBMC) molecular simulation using the Materials Studio program52 indicate that Gsp can interact with the MOF structure and adopt a favorable configuration with the MOF cavity. The simulation results indicate that Gsp interacts with the framework through hydrogen bonding between Gsp -OH group and the $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\ell}}_{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ group of the ligands: H-bonding distances are $2.18\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ $(\mathrm{O}{\cdot}{\bullet}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{N})$ and $2.{\overset{-}{3}}4\mathrm{\AA}$ (O-H•••N (Figure S12). These significant interactions position the Gsp -OH groups in proximity of the MOF $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ centers (Figure S12).  The interaction of Gsp with ${\mathrm{Yb-NH}}_{2}$ - TPDC was determined to be thermodynamically stable, with a stabilization energy of $-42.5\mathrm{kcal/mol}$ (Figure S13).  Based on the experimental results and CBMC simulation results, we can reasonably conclude that Gsp can enter the MOF cavities and interact favorably with the MOF structure, which is a prerequisite for $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ sensitization via both of our hypothesized mechanisms.
We next studied the kinetics of $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL of $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC in response to Gsp. Specifically, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of Gsp/acetone solution (25 $\upmu{\mathrm{g/mL}},50\upmu{\mathrm{g/mL}},75\upmu{\mathrm{g/mL}},100\upmu{\mathrm{g/mL}})$ was added to $2{\mathrm{~mL}}$ of acetone suspension containing $2.5~\mathrm{mg}$ of activated $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC powder in a standard macro fluorescence cuvette. $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL was immediately monitored for $300\mathrm{min}$ under $485\ \mathrm{nm}$ excitation light (Supporting Information, Page S20). Figure 4A shows the evolution of $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL intensity at $976~\mathrm{nm}$ as a function of time, where data points at $0\mathrm{min}$ represent PL intensity measured immediately after the addition of Gsp solutions. Upon addition of Gsp, $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ emission gradually increased over $300\mathrm{min}$ . The slow increase of PL intensity is likely due to the hindered diffusion of the bulky Gsp within activated Yb- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ -TPDC, which is consistent with the observed slow decrease of Gsp concentration in the presence of ${\mathrm{Yb}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ - TPDC as monitored by solution UV-vis spectroscopy (Figure S9). With an equal amount of incubation time, stronger $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL intensity correlates with higher Gsp concentration in the $25\mathrm{~-~}100~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ range (Figure 4B). At $300~\mathrm{{min}}$ , $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL intensity follows a linear dependence with Gsp concentration between $25~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ and 100 $\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ (Figure 4B inset), and therefore is suitable for quantification of Gsp in this concentration range. No appreciable $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ emission was observed in the absence of Gsp. The limit of detection was determined to be $25~\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ ( $\left[\sim32\:\mathrm{ppm}\right.$ in acetone solution) with a $\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}$ of 7. No reliable $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ emission was detected when solutions of lower concentrations were tested. We note that the actual minimum detectable Gsp concentration was $8.3~\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ $\left(\sim11\mathrm{ppm}\right)$ in acetone solution), because the $1\mathrm{mL}$ of $25~\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ Gsp solution was diluted into $3\mathrm{mL}$ in cuvette. Despite the relatively slow response, ${\mathrm{Yb}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ - TPDC is a promising sensor for practical detection and quantification of Gsp in terms of its limit of detection.
Interference from background molecules present in realistic samples, such as cottonseed oil and cotton meals, may hamper Gsp detection and quantification. To evaluate the selectivity of ${\mathrm{Yb-NH}}_{2}$ - TPDC as a sensor for Gsp, we further investigated whether $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL excited at $485\mathrm{nm}$ can be turned on by substances commonly present in the context of Gsp detection (Supporting Information, Page S20). Refined cottonseed oil solution $\mathrm{'100mg/mL})$ , palmitic acid solution $\left(1~\mathrm{mg/mL}\right)$ , linoleic acid solution $\left(1~\mathrm{mg/mL}\right)$ , and $\scriptstyle{\mathfrak{a}}.$ - tocopherol solution $\mathrm{{(1mg/mL)}}$ ) did not induce $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL (white bars in Figure 4C). Presumably, $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL cannot be sensitized by these substances because they do not absorb visible light (Figure S14 and S15) or they cannot modulate the absorption of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC linkers. Apart from producing interfering signals, interferents could also block the interactions between the target analyte and sensor. For instance, background molecules could adsorb to $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC more favorably than Gsp and prevent Gsp from switching on $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ luminescence. To assess the Gsp detection performance of ${\mathrm{Yb-NH}}_{2}$ - TPDC in the presence of background substances, $\mathrm{Yb^{3+}P L}$ intensity at $976\mathrm{nm}$ in response to Gsp $\mathrm{\langle100\upmug/mL\rangle}$ without interferents was compared to PL intensity in the presence of cottonseed oil (100 $\mathrm{mg/mL})$ , palmitic acid $\mathrm{{(1mg/mL)}}$ , linoleic acid $\mathrm{{(1mg/mL)}}$ , and $\mathfrak{a}$ - tocopherol $\left(1~\mathrm{mg/mL}\right)$ (shaded bars in Figure 4C), respectively. Although the concentrations of all tested interferents were at least 10 times higher than Gsp, the PL intensities of $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC are largely unaffected. Overall, the $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL of $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC is selectively turned on by Gsp.
Another key advantage of Yb- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC is its recyclability. After every fluorescence titration experiment, $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC was regenerated by simply washing with acetone to remove adsorbed
Gsp. Once regenerated, the MOF sample exhibited no $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ emission under $485\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, and could be reused to detect Gsp for at least four cycles (Figure S16A) without significant change in PL intensity. We further note that during this recyclability test ${\mathrm{Yb-NH}}_{2}$ - TPDC maintained its crystallinity as evidenced by the largely unaltered sample PXRD patterns (Figure S16B), indicating Yb$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC is stable in Gsp solution.
As discussed earlier, two mechanisms are likely responsible for the “turn-on” response of Yb- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC PL at $485\mathrm{nm}$ excitation in the presence of Gsp: i) Gsp can independently function as an effective sensitizer for $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ luminescence, or ii) Gsp can red-shift the absorption band of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC linker, the integral sensitizer in Yb- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC, by formation of a Schiff base with a larger $\pi$ system. The fact that Yb- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC is a recyclable sensor for Gsp detection suggests the latter mechanism involving covalent bond formation is unlikely. To further confirm the operative mechanism, $Y_{b-N O_{2}}$ - TPDC was synthesized using $2^{\prime}$ -nitro-1,1':4',1''-terphenyl-4,4''- dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\tilde{(H_{2}-N O_{2}}}$ -TPDC). Yb-NO2-TPDC and $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ - TPDC are isoreticular, as confirmed by PXRD (Figure S17). Lacking $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\ell}}_{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ groups, $\mathrm{Yb-NO}_{2}$ -TPDC cannot react with Gsp to form a Schiff base. In the absence of Gsp, the $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -TPDC linker does not sensitize $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL at any excitation wavelengths (Figure 5A). This may be due to the presence of the electron-withdrawing $\left.-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right.$ group in the $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -TPDC linker, which may lead to rapid nonradiative relaxation from its excited states, or mismatched triplet state energy level that renders energy transfer from the triplet state of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -TPDC to $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ inefficient. Incubating $\mathrm{Yb-NO}_{2}$ -TPDC with Gsp solution, however, led to strong $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL with excitation wavelengths spanning from $350\mathrm{nm}$ to $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 5B, Figure S18, S19). Significantly, these experiments indicate, for the first time, that Gsp is an effective sensitizer for ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}{\mathrm{PL}},$ which was further supported by comparing the Yb PL quantum yield $(Q_{V b})$ values of $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC under $485\mathrm{nm}$ excitation light in the presence and in the absence of Gsp. The $Q_{Y b}$ of $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC in the absence of Gsp was determined to be $1.69\pm0.08\left(10^{-2}\right)\%,$ while in the presence of $25\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ and $100\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Gsp $Q_{Y b}$ values increased dramatically to $1.94\pm0.08$ $(10^{-1})\%$ and $8.31\pm0.08$ $(10^{-1})\%$ respectively.   We note further that no sign of Schiff base formation was observed after reacting $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC with Gsp using our previously reported method (Figure S20, and S21, Supporting Information Page S27- 28).45 Therefore, we conclude that the “turn-on” response of $\mathrm{Yb^{3+}P L}$ at $485\mathrm{nm}$ excitation is mainly due to the “antenna effect” of Gsp. As antenna, Gsp can sensitize $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL of both $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC and Yb$\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -TPDC at excitation wavelengths greater than $525~\mathrm{nm}$ , while Gsp in solution does not exhibit absorption above $480\mathrm{nm}$ . The large discrepancy between the Gsp excitation range in the MOF and its absorption band in solution is reminiscent of our previous observation with $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -TPDC linker and may also be attributed to confinement effects.
To determine whether Gsp could conceivably serve as a sensitizer for $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ from an energetic standpoint, we applied density functional theory (DFT) using the Gaussian 09 program53 to calculate its electronic structure. The structure optimization and highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO)-lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy of both ligand and Gsp was calculated using the $\mathrm{B}3\mathrm{LYP}/6{\cdot}31\mathrm{G}+\left(\mathrm{d},\mathrm{p}\right)$ basis set (Figure S22A) and an energy level diagram illustrating the potential energy transfer processes that could occur in this system was derived. We can conclude from the calculations that both Gsp and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC could potentially sensitize $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ (Figure S22B). However, under $485~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, only Gsp-based sensitization would be possible. In that case, the mechanism of sensitization could be either via energy transfer from Gsp to $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ or via energy transfer from the Gsp singlet state $\begin{array}{r}{\rightarrow\mathrm{NH}_{2}.}\end{array}$ - TPDC triplet state $\rightarrow$ Gsp triplet state ${\rightarrow}\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ (Figure S22C). While determination of the precise mechanism of energy transfer is beyond the scope of this report, we can reasonably conclude, from our collection of experimental and computational studies, that Gsp sensitizes $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ luminescence via the “antenna effect”.
With strong evidence supporting that Gsp is an effective sensitizer for ${\Upsilon}{\mathsf{b}}^{3+}~{\mathrm{PL}},$ we further explored other $\mathrm{Ln}^{+3}$ materials for Gsp detection. Since Gsp can form complexes with $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions in solution,44 we were particularly interested to test whether simple and commercially available $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ salts can be used as “turn-on” sensors for Gsp. Indeed, ${\mathrm{Yb}}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ solution exhibited intense $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL upon addition of Gsp. The excitation-emission map of an ${\mathrm{YbCl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solution after 5 min incubation with Gsp (Figure S23) showed that $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL can be observed upon excitation from $330\mathrm{nm}$ to $480\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Compared to $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC, the response time of ${\mathrm{Yb}}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ is significantly faster, likely because Gsp molecules can readily access and coordinate $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ ions in solution without overcoming the diffusion barriers posed by MOF pores. To determine the detection range of ${\mathrm{YbCl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{\cdot}$ , emission spectra were collected 5 minutes after $1\mathrm{mL}$ of Gsp/acetone solution $(0\upmu\mathrm{g/mL},0.5\upmu\mathrm{g/mL},1\upmu\mathrm{g/mL},$ 3 µg/mL, 5 µg/mL, 7 µg/mL, 10 µg/mL, 25 µg/mL, $50\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL},$ ) was added to $2\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{YbCl_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O/D M F}$ solution $\mathrm{(1~mg/mL)}$ (Figure S24). No emission was observed for the sample with acetone blank, while $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ emission was observed for all samples containing Gsp. The limit of detection was determined to be $0.5\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\left(0.64\upmathrm{p p m}\right)$ with a $\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}$ of 10. The PL intensity at $976~\mathrm{nm}$ follows a linear dependence with the concentration of Gsp solution between 0.5 $\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ and $10~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ and approached saturation at higher concentrations (Figure S25). In addition, we discovered that the $\mathrm{Nd^{3+}}$ PL of $\mathrm{NdCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at $1056\ \mathrm{nm}$ can also be used for Gsp detection in a similar fashion (Figure S26 and S27). Yb- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC and ${\mathrm{Yb}}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ are complimentary and allow for detection and quantification of Gsp at different concentration ranges: from 0.5 $\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ to $10~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ using ${\mathrm{Yb}}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{\cdot}$ , and from 25 to $100\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ using $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC. Compared to $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC, $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ salts PL is more easily affected by interferents as indicated by interference experiments using ${\mathrm{YbCl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL intensity of ${\mathrm{YbCl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at $976\ \mathrm{nm}$ in response to Gsp $\left(25\mathrm{\upmug/mL}\right)$ was enhanced in the presence of refined cottonseed oil $\mathrm{(100~mg/mL)}$ , palmitic acid $\mathrm{(1~\mg/mL)}$ , and $\mathfrak{a}$ -tocopherol $\mathrm{~(~1~~mg/mL)~}$ , but dampened by linoleic acid $\mathrm{{(1\mg/mL)}}$ (Figure S28). This is likely caused by the coordination of interferents to $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ ions in solution, leading to either better protection from quenching or more quenching of $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ PL. To summarize, $\mathrm{Yb-NH}_{2}$ -TPDC has improved resistance to interference and can be advantageous for integration into solid state devices that are recyclable, whereas ${\mathrm{YbCl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ provides a simple way to rapidly and sensitively detect Gsp in solution. We further compared the performance of the $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ sensors reported in this work with other representative techniques for gossypol detection in Table S1. The Gsp detection methods we introduce have a limit of detection and  response time on par with, or even superior to, those of other spectroscopic methods.
Compared with the previously reported luminescence “turn-off” sensor, the sensors described herein have much higher $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{sv}}$ values and, therefore, are more sensitive. The luminescence “turn-on” also provides reliable and straightforward readout that requires little additional data interpretation. In addition, the vast number of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}.$ - based materials offers tremendous potential for further improvement of sensor performance.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we discovered that Gsp, a toxic molecule that concerns the cotton industry, can effectively sensitize $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Nd^{3+}}$ NIR PL. Leveraging this discovery, we demonstrate the first examples of luminescence “turn-on” detection of Gsp using $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ MOFs and ${\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}$ salts and achieve highly selective, fast-responding, and sensitive sensing of Gsp in acetone solution with a concentration of $0.5-100\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ . We envision that this general sensing approach will be applicable to the detection of a wide variety of aromatic molecules, including pharmaceuticals, pesticides, and other contaminants that may be present in water and food. A large variety of known $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ MOFs, complexes and salts may be utilized to optimize sensor performance. Our future efforts in this area include i) identification of other important analytes that can be effectively detected using this approach, and ii) accordingly, rational design and optimization of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ -based  luminescence “turn-on” sensors via careful control of their structural and compositional parameters.
## EXPERIMENTAL METHODS
Materials. All purchased chemicals were used without further purification except where otherwise noted. Refined cottonseed oil, Gsp, palmitic acid, linoleic acid, $\mathfrak{a}$ -tocopherol were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Nanopure water $\left(18.1~\mathrm{M}\Omega\right)$ was obtained from a Barnstead DiamondTM water purification system.
Powder X-ray Diffraction. PXRD patterns were collected using a Bruker AXS D8 Discover powder diffractometer at $40\mathrm{kV_{\it\Omega}}$ , $40\mathrm{mA}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}\ K a,$ $(\uplambda=1.5406\tilde{\mathrm{A}}$ ) from 3 to $45^{\circ}$ at a step size of $0.02^{\mathrm{o}}$ . The data were analyzed using the EVA program from the Bruker Powder Analysis Software package. The simulated powder patterns were calculated using Mercury 3.8 and CIF files.
Single Crystal $\mathsf{\pmb X}$ -ray Diffraction. SCXRD experiments were performed on a Bruker X8 Prospector Ultra diffractometer equipped with an Apex II CCD detector and an $\mathrm{I}\upmu\mathrm{S}$ micro-focus Cu Kα X-ray source $(\lambda=1.54178\mathring\mathrm{A}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ). Data were collected at $230\mathrm{K}$ under $\Nu_{2}$ flow and integrated by Bruker program SAINT. Empirical absorption correction was applied using program SADABS. The structure was solved with direct method using SHELXT and refined by full-matrix least-squares on $\mathrm{F}^{2}$ using SHELXL54-55 in Olex2.56
Thermogravimetric Analyses. TGA were performed using a TGA Q500 thermal analysis system under a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere from room temperature to $650^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a ramping rate of $5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ .
UV-vis Spectroscopy. Solution UV−vis absorption spectra were collected using an Agilent 8453 UV−vis spectrometer equipped with deuterium and tungsten lamps.
Photoluminescence Experiments. PL data were collected using a Horiba Jobin-Yvon NanoLog spectrofluorometer with a 450
W xenon source, double excitation monochromators, and a Symphony II InGaAs array detector. Excitation gratings were blazed at $330\mathrm{nm}$ with 1200 grooves $\mathrm{'mm}$ and emission gratings blazed at $780~\mathrm{nm}$ with 100 grooves/ $\mathrm{{\dot{m}m}}$ . All measurements were obtained with an $830\mathrm{nm}$ long-pass filter at ambient temperature. ExcitationEmission maps of MOF samples were measured in an integration sphere with solid MOF samples stored under acetone in quartz capillary tubes. Excitation-Emission map of $\mathrm{YbCl}_{3}{\bullet}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and all sensing experiments were performed using a standard fluorescence macro cuvette equipped with a PTFE stopper (Fireflysci type 21 macro cuvette, light path: $10\ \mathrm{mm}\times10\ \mathrm{mm}$ ). Detailed experiment conditions and parameters can be found in the supporting information.
Synthesis of Yb-NH2-TPDC. Stock solutions of $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -TPDC (0.05 M) and YbCl3∙6H2O (0.05 M) and 2,6-difluorobenzoic acid (1 M) in DMF were prepared. A mixture of concentrated hydrochloric acid/DMF $\left(1/2.3~\mathrm{v/v}\right)$ was prepared. To a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ Pyrex vial was added $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TPDC solution $(1.5\mathrm{\mL},0.05\mathrm{\M})$ ), ${\mathrm{YbCl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ 1 $\mathrm{\langle0.75~mL}$ , $0.05\mathrm{~M~},$ ), $^{2,6}$ -difluorobenzoic acid $\mathrm{.0.75~mL}$ , 1 M), nanopure $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ $\mathrm{~(0.15~}\mathrm{~mL)}$ and concentrated hydrochloric acid/DMF $(0.2\mathrm{mL})$ ) sequentially. The vial was tightly capped and heated in a $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ isothermal oven for 14 hours. The vial was then removed from oven and cooled down naturally to room temperature. Crystals were collected after centrifugation and washed with fresh DMF $\mathbf{3x},~2~\mathrm{mL}$ each time). Yield: $15\ \mathrm{mg}$ . Formula: $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathrm{Yb}_{6}(\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mathrm{C_{20}H_{14}N_{2}O_{4}})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]_{\bullet}2(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{8}N}),17\mathrm{DMF},6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.}\end{array}$ Anal. calcd. $(\%)$ : C, 43.76; H, 5.27; N, 9.04; Found $(\%)$ : C, 43.09; H, 4.68; N, 9.32.
Synthesis of $Y_{b-N O_{2}}$ -TPDC. Stock solutions of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{\cdotNO}_{2}$ -TPDC (0.05 M) and $\mathrm{YbCl_{3}.6H_{2}O\left(0.05M\right)}$ and 2,6-difluorobenzoic acid (1 M) in DMF were prepared. To a $4~\mathrm{mL}$ Pyrex vial equipped with a PTFE cap was added $\mathrm{\DeltaH}_{2}{\mathrm{-NO}}_{2}$ -TPDC solution $(0.3~\mathrm{mL},0.05~\mathrm{M})$ , YbCl3 $6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(0.15\mathrm{mL},0.05\mathrm{M}\right)$ , 2,6-difluorobenzoic acid $(0.25\mathrm{mL},$ ${\bf1}\mathrm{{M})}$ sequentially. The vial was tightly capped and heated in a 100 $^\circ C$ isothermal oven for 40 hours. The vial was then removed from oven and cooled down naturally to room temperature. Crystals were collected after centrifugation and washed with fresh DMF $\mathbf{3x},\mathbf{1mL}$ each time). Yield: $2.3\mathrm{mg}$ .
